Counterevidence and repair of the required valuation step. Unramifiedness of the special-point field is not needed for the infinite-order argument. Retain an arbitrary uniformizer \(\varpi\) of \(L=E(sK_0)_v\), let \(e=e(L/\mathbb Q_p)\), and normalize \(v_p(p)=1\). Since \(\mu_x\) is defined over \(L\), \[v_p\!\left(\chi\!\left(N_{L/\mathbb Q_p}(\mu_x(\varpi))\right)\right)
 =\frac{1}{e}\sum_{\sigma:L\hookrightarrow\overline\mathbb Q_p}
       \langle\chi,{}^\sigma\mu_x\rangle.\] All conjugate cocharacters are dominant for the rational Borel. On each nontrivial adjoint factor there is a positive root with positive pairing for at least one summand, and all summands for that root are nonnegative. Thus the displayed valuation is positive. The compact right side of Equation (7) has zero character valuations, so the projection of \(q\) has infinite order. Equation (6) transfers this to the Frobenius element. This reasoning uses compactness, not maximality of that compact subgroup.

